<Doc frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p>Why Agent Guard exists, what other scanners leave open, how a scan works from input to report, and the code and algorithms behind it.</p>

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>Coding agents now install skills (<code>SKILL.md</code> bundles), follow instruction files (<code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, Cursor rules) and connect to MCP servers. Each one runs with the agent's access: your files, your shell, your credentials and the network. The model reads their text as instructions, and some of it runs before the model sees it (Claude Code's <code>!</code> commands and skill hooks).</p>
  <p>That makes them a supply chain with very little review. A skill can tell the agent to "install a prerequisite" that is malware, hide instructions in invisible Unicode or an HTML comment, or read <code>~/.ssh</code> and post it to a webhook. Often the attack is plain language, which no antivirus signature matches. A server can change its tools after you approve it (a rug pull). A skill can depend on a GitHub account, package or domain that anyone can now register (SkillJacking: AIR Security counted 925 such skills).</p>
  <p>The question to answer, before anything is installed, enabled or merged: <strong>is it safe to give this skill or MCP server to my agent, and if not, exactly why, and how do I fix it?</strong></p>

  <h2>Gaps in current tools</h2>
  <p>Checked against each project's own documentation on 2026-10-30. These tools change quickly; see their pages for the current state.</p>
  <div class="corners"><div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it covers</th><th>What it leaves open</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/snyk/agent-scan">Snyk Agent Scan</a></td><td>MCP servers, skills and agent configs on your machine</td><td>Starts stdio MCP servers by running the commands in your config (it asks first; CI needs <code>--dangerously-run-mcp-servers</code>). Sends configs, tool descriptions and skill files to the Snyk API and needs a Snyk token. Its docs don't describe pinning tool definitions between scans.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/cisco-ai-defense/skill-scanner">Cisco skill-scanner</a></td><td>Agent skills: static, bytecode and dataflow analyzers run locally</td><td>Skills only, no MCP configs or servers. The LLM, VirusTotal and AI Defense analyzers need API keys and send content out.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/cisco-ai-defense/mcp-scanner">Cisco mcp-scanner</a></td><td>MCP servers, tools, prompts and resources</td><td>MCP only, no skills. Connects to live servers and launches stdio servers to read their tools. The LLM and AI Defense engines need API keys.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.openclaw.ai/blog/virustotal-partnership">ClawHub + VirusTotal</a></td><td>Skills published to ClawHub, at publish time and daily</td><td>Only covers that one registry, and uploads bundles to VirusTotal. OpenClaw says a skill that uses natural language to instruct an agent to do something malicious "won't trigger a virus signature".</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div></div>

  <h2>How Agent Guard closes them</h2>
  <p>The gaps the tools above share, each with Agent Guard's answer beside it.</p>
  <div class="advice pairs corners">
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Scanning by running</h3><p>Listing a server's tools by starting it executes the code you are trying to judge.</p></div>
    <div class="fix"><h3 class="cell-h">Agent Guard</h3><p>Parse-only. A process-wide audit hook blocks process creation; the only exception is a hardened, read-only <code>git</code> call in PR mode. Tool definitions come from manifests, <code>server.json</code> and source code. Listing a live server is opt-in, read-only and for remote servers only; stdio servers are never started.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Your setup leaves the machine</h3><p>Cloud analysis needs an account and sends what you have installed to a third party.</p></div>
    <div class="fix"><h3 class="cell-h">Agent Guard</h3><p>Offline by default, no account, no telemetry. Network features are opt-in flags, and every report lists the ones used. In the browser, pasted and dropped input never leaves the device (a CI test asserts zero network requests).</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Split coverage</h3><p>Skills, instruction files, client configs, tool metadata and server code are checked by different tools, or not at all.</p></div>
    <div class="fix"><h3 class="cell-h">Agent Guard</h3><p>One engine for skills, instruction files, MCP client configs, MCP tool metadata and server code, across Claude, Cursor, VS Code, Copilot, Codex, Gemini CLI, Windsurf, Zed, Cline and OpenClaw.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Risks that span components</h3><p>A server that reads private files and another that sends data out each look fine when scanned alone.</p></div>
    <div class="fix"><h3 class="cell-h">Agent Guard</h3><p>Toxic flows: capability labels on every component, combined across everything active in one agent session, reported as a concrete path.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Signatures miss language</h3><p>Malicious instructions are text, not malware.</p></div>
    <div class="fix"><h3 class="cell-h">Agent Guard</h3><p>Instruction-layer rules (overrides, concealment, approval bypass, persistence, paraphrased download-and-run lures), hidden-content detection with decoding and rescanning, and an optional LLM judge for paraphrase that can never raise a finding to CRITICAL.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Known bypasses</h3><p><a href="https://blog.trailofbits.com/2026/06/03/the-sorry-state-of-skill-distribution/">Trail of Bits</a> got four malicious skills past ClawHub, the skills.sh scanners and Cisco's skill-scanner (June 2026); the <a href="https://labs.cloudsecurityalliance.org/research/csa-research-note-ai-agent-skill-scanner-bypass-20260610-csa/">Cloud Security Alliance</a> found that obfuscation and splitting content across files degraded commercial scanners.</p></div>
    <div class="fix"><h3 class="cell-h">Agent Guard</h3><p>The published techniques are reproduced inertly in the benchmark and covered: blank-line padding, a script inside an Office document, Python bytecode that does not match its source, registry redirection, encoded and split payloads (linked across files of a skill). What is still missed is listed in <a href="../limitations/">Limitations</a>.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">One point in time</h3><p>A scan at install says nothing about the server you connect to next week, or about dependencies anyone can take over.</p></div>
    <div class="fix"><h3 class="cell-h">Agent Guard</h3><p><code>lock</code> / <code>verify</code> pins skill files and normalized tool definitions and shows any change as a diff. <code>--check-hijackable</code> finds dependencies anyone can claim. A signed IOC feed flags known-bad hashes, domains and publishers.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">False assurance</h3><p>"Benign" or "no issues" reads as safe, even when parts of the input were never analyzed.</p></div>
    <div class="fix"><h3 class="cell-h">Agent Guard</h3><p>A clean scan says "No findings from N rules (rule pack vX)", never "safe". Analyzers that did not run and content skipped by limits are listed. An analyzer error exits with code 2, never a silent pass. Benchmark misses are published.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Objectives</h2>
  <div class="advice cells corners">
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Answer before install</h3><p>Whether a skill, instruction file or MCP server is risky, why (evidence at file and line), and how to fix it.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Never execute what is scanned</h3><p>On a laptop, in CI or on a server.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Private by default</h3><p>Offline, no account, no telemetry.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">One engine everywhere</h3><p>The CLI, the GitHub Action, the browser and the web server produce the same report from the same input.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Honest and repeatable</h3><p>Never "safe"; the same input and rule pack give a byte-identical report.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Measured accuracy</h3><p>CRITICAL precision of at least 0.95, a benign false-positive rate of at most 0.02 at HIGH, and 100 skills plus 20 server configs in under 10 seconds, all published on the <a href="../../benchmarks/">benchmarks</a> page.</p></div>
    <div><h3 class="cell-h">Standards-aligned</h3><p>Every rule maps to the OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications, OWASP MCP Top 10, OWASP Agentic Skills Top 10, OWASP LLM Top 10, the NSA MCP guidance and CWE, with an AIVSS score.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Methodology</h2>
  <p>Every scan runs the same seven stages.</p>

  <Pipeline />

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Discover</strong> agent configuration, skills and instruction files (or load the path you give).</li>
    <li><strong>Load safely:</strong> size, count and depth limits; archive-bomb caps; symlinks, junctions and path escapes are refused and reported; YAML is safe-loaded with anchors, aliases and custom tags rejected.</li>
    <li><strong>Normalize:</strong> Unicode is NFKC-normalized and look-alike characters are folded, with offsets mapped back to the original; invisible characters are removed; encoded blobs (base64, hex, percent, gzip) are decoded to depth 3 and rescanned; hidden Markdown (comments, off-screen text, alt text, CSS-hidden elements) is extracted.</li>
    <li><strong>Analyze</strong> with independent analyzers: skill spec and structure, hidden content, data-defined pattern rules, MCP configuration, MCP metadata, code (taint from tool parameters, followed into helper functions of the same server: Python via the standard <code>ast</code> module, JS/TS by pattern), supply chain and policy. Two optional analyzers need native extensions and are reported as skipped without them: tree-sitter taint for JS/TS handlers and shell scripts (<code>agentguard[code]</code>), and YARA rules over the raw bytes of bundled files (<code>agentguard[yara]</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Correlate:</strong> deduplicate, escalate findings found inside hidden content by one severity, combine credential access with egress (also across linked files of a skill: a file one script writes and another reads, one script running another, or an import), and build the <strong>capability graph</strong> for toxic flows.</li>
    <li><strong>Score</strong> each finding (AIVSS v0.8, pluggable) and apply suppressions, baselines and policy.</li>
    <li><strong>Report</strong> as terminal output, JSON, SARIF 2.1.0, Markdown or a single-file HTML report.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>The engine is a pure function of its inputs: <code>scan(tree, options) → report</code>. It never touches the filesystem or network itself, so the same input and rule pack always produce the same report, and the same engine runs in the browser under Pyodide.</p>

  <h3>How rules are built and checked</h3>
  <div class="advice cells corners">
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">Rules are data</h4><p>Each rule is YAML in the rule pack with an unsafe and a safe fixture. CI runs every fixture, so a rule that stops firing, or starts firing on its safe example, fails the build.</p></div>
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">A benchmark with held-out rounds</h4><p>123 synthetic items (76 attacks, 47 benign look-alikes) and 163 real benign skills and servers from eight vendor repositories at pinned commits. Each tuning round is first measured on items the rules have not seen.</p></div>
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">Primary sources, dated</h4><p>Specs, client config paths and attack techniques are checked against vendor documentation or the original research, and each check is recorded with its date in <code>docs/sources.md</code>.</p></div>
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">Safety and determinism tests</h4><p>No process may start across the whole fixture corpus; the same input must give byte-identical output; the browser scanner must make zero requests for pasted input.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h3>Severity rules</h3>
  <div class="advice cells three corners">
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">CRITICAL needs proof</h4><p>High confidence and deterministic evidence; a lower-confidence critical match is reported as high (tagged <code>capped-by-confidence</code>).</p></div>
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">Hidden means worse</h4><p>Matches inside hidden or encoded content are raised one level (tagged <code>hidden</code>).</p></div>
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">The judge is capped</h4><p>Results from the optional LLM judge can never be critical.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Architecture</h2>
  <p>One pure-Python engine, wrapped by three hosts. The hosts do the I/O; the engine only turns a file tree into a report.</p>
  <Architecture />

  <div class="corners"><div class="table-scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Engine</td><td><code>src/agentguard/core</code>: no filesystem, network or clock access. Loads the rule pack, runs the analyzers, correlates, scores and renders JSON, SARIF, Markdown, HTML and CycloneDX. <code>schemas/report.v1.json</code> is the only contract between the engine and every UI.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CLI and GitHub Action</td><td>Finds agent configuration on the machine, loads folders, repositories and configs under safety limits, writes and checks lockfiles, and runs the opt-in network checks and LLM judge. In pull requests it scans only the components a change touches and reads its own configuration from the base commit.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Browser scanner</td><td>The <a href="../../scan/">/scan</a> page (see <a href="../web-scanner/">Web scanner</a>) runs the engine under Pyodide in a Web Worker. Pasted text and dropped folders or <code>.zip</code> files are scanned on the device; public GitHub links are pinned to a commit and fetched from GitHub.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agent Guard Web server</td><td>For links to GitHub, npm, PyPI, the MCP Registry or a remote MCP server. The dispatcher pins each link to an exact commit or version (registry digests verified), downloads it through an allowlisted, SSRF-hardened fetcher, and scans it in a throwaway container with no network, a read-only filesystem and no privileges (optionally under gVisor). Reports are stored with an unlisted permalink, keyed by reference, rule pack and engine version. The site reaches it through an <code>/api/</code> rewrite.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shared data</td><td>Rule pack (<code>rules/</code>), standards crosswalk (<code>mappings/</code>), signed IOC feed (<code>intel/</code>) and the benchmark corpus (<code>bench/</code>).</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div></div>

  <h2>The code</h2>
  <p>Agent Guard is mostly Python, and much of its detection logic is data. The engine's own code is pure Python and needs only Pydantic, <code>regex</code> and PyYAML, all of which have WebAssembly builds, so it runs unchanged in the browser. Line counts are from October 2026.</p>
  <div class="facts corners"><dl class="report-facts">
    <div><dt>Engine, CLI, network</dt><dd><span class="v">15,000</span><span class="sub">lines of Python in <code>src/agentguard</code></span></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Web server</dt><dd><span class="v">2,800</span><span class="sub">lines of Python in <code>web/</code></span></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Rule pack</dt><dd><span class="v">2,500</span><span class="sub">lines of YAML for 173 rules</span></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Tests</dt><dd><span class="v">206</span><span class="sub">Python test functions, plus two fixtures per rule</span></dd></div>
  </dl></div>

  <h3>Languages and libraries</h3>
  <div class="corners"><div class="table-scroll"><table class="code-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Python 3.12+</td><td><code>src/agentguard</code>, <code>web/</code></td><td>The engine, CLI, opt-in network checks, LLM judge and web server. Five runtime dependencies: Pydantic (data model and JSON Schemas), <code>regex</code> (matching with timeouts), PyYAML (safe loading), Typer and Rich (CLI). Optional: tree-sitter, <code>yara-python</code>, the Anthropic SDK. The server adds FastAPI, Uvicorn, Redis and Postgres.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>YAML</td><td><code>rules/</code>, <code>mappings/</code></td><td>The rule pack as data: what each rule matches, its severity and confidence, standards mappings and remediation. 17 files.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>YARA</td><td><code>rules/yara/</code></td><td>Byte-level patterns for bundled binaries (optional analyzer).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Python in WebAssembly</td><td>the <code>/scan</code> page</td><td>Pyodide runs the same engine in a Web Worker, so pasted and dropped files never leave the device.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Astro, HTML, CSS</td><td><code>site/</code></td><td>The static website. Charts are SVG computed at build time; the Markdown pages pass through a small build step that frames tables and code. No inline scripts or styles, so a strict Content-Security-Policy holds.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>JavaScript</td><td><code>site/public/js</code>, <code>site/scripts</code></td><td>The scan page and its Web Workers, the proof-of-work solver, the rotating globe (Canvas 2D, no libraries) and build scripts.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dockerfiles, GitHub Actions</td><td><code>web/docker</code>, <code>.github/</code></td><td>The scanner sandbox image (non-root, no network) and CI.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div></div>

  <h3>Algorithms</h3>
  <p>Paths are under <code>src/agentguard/core/</code> unless shown otherwise.</p>
  <div class="corners"><div class="table-scroll"><table class="code-table">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Algorithm</th><th scope="col">What it does here</th><th scope="col">Code</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>NFKC + confusable folding</td><td>Normalizes Unicode and folds look-alike letters (Cyrillic "а" to Latin "a"), keeping an offset map so findings still point at the original line and column</td><td><code>normalize/unicode.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Recursive decoding</td><td>Decodes base64, hex, percent, <code>\x</code> escapes and gzip inside base64, up to three layers deep, and scans what it finds again</td><td><code>normalize/decode.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Regex with timeouts</td><td>Runs the data-defined pattern rules with a 0.5 s limit per match, so no pattern can hang a scan</td><td><code>analyzers/regex_rules.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>AST taint analysis</td><td>Follows Python tool parameters through assignments, f-strings, formatting and string methods, and into helper functions up to three deep, to a shell, process arguments, <code>eval</code>, deserialization, file paths, URLs, SQL or templates</td><td><code>analyzers/pytaint.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tree-sitter taint</td><td>The same for JavaScript/TypeScript handlers and shell scripts (optional), capped at 1 MB per file and 200,000 nodes per walk</td><td><code>analyzers/treesitter.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Breadth-first link search</td><td>Links a skill's files (a path one writes and another reads, one script running another, imports) and searches up to three hops, so a credential read and a network send in different files are joined</td><td><code>correlate/links.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Capability graph</td><td>Labels every component, groups them by agent session and reports dangerous combinations, such as the lethal trifecta, as a path</td><td><code>correlate/flows.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Damerau–Levenshtein distance</td><td>Optimal string alignment with early exit, to spot typosquatted skill names and near-identical tool names</td><td><code>analyzers/heuristics.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Secret patterns + Shannon entropy</td><td>22 patterns (API keys and tokens, private keys, JWTs, bearer tokens, passwords in URLs), plus at least 3.5 bits of entropy per character over 16+ characters in secret-named fields</td><td><code>secrets.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Typed redaction</td><td>Every snippet in a report has a type that only redaction can produce: the first 4 characters plus a short hash</td><td><code>redact.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Canonical JSON + SHA-256</td><td>Sorted-key JSON (close to RFC 8785), hashed for lockfile entries of tool definitions (rug pulls) and the web server's report cache keys</td><td><code>lock.py</code>, <code>cachekey.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ed25519, pure Python</td><td>RFC 8032 signature check of the IOC feed with no crypto library, so it also works under Pyodide</td><td><code>intel/ed25519.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>AIVSS v0.8 score</td><td>AARS = (10 − CVSS) × (factors ÷ 10) × threat, then AIVSS = (CVSS + AARS) × mitigation, for every finding</td><td><code>scoring/aivss.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bounded archive streaming</td><td>Extracts zip and tar in memory with compression-ratio and size caps, dropping links and path escapes</td><td><code>archive.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Process audit hook</td><td>A process-wide Python audit hook refuses to start any process, so nothing scanned can run</td><td><code>src/agentguard/io/guard.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>SSRF-hardened HTTP</td><td>Resolves DNS first, allows only globally routable addresses, pins the IP and re-checks every redirect, for the opt-in checks and the web server's fetcher</td><td><code>src/agentguard/net/safe_http.py</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Proof of work</td><td>The browser finds a nonce whose SHA-256 starts with N zero bits for an HMAC-signed, single-use challenge: bot protection with no CAPTCHA</td><td><code>web/…/pow.py</code>, <code>site/public/js/pow.js</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Precision, recall, F1</td><td>With the false-positive rate, per category and severity, to measure every rule change on the benchmark</td><td><code>src/agentguard/bench/runner.py</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div></div>

  <h3>How the code is tested</h3>
  <div class="advice cells three corners">
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">Unit, fixture and safety tests</h4><p>206 Python test functions for the engine and web server, plus an unsafe and a safe fixture for each of the 173 rules. No process may start across the whole fixture corpus.</p></div>
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">CI matrix</h4><p>Linux, macOS and Windows on Python 3.12 and 3.14; the engine again under Pyodide; the web server's isolation tests against real containers; browser tests with Playwright.</p></div>
    <div><h4 class="cell-h">Benchmark gate</h4><p>CI fails if the committed benchmark results are stale or a change makes any item worse.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Toxic flows</h2>
  <p>Every skill, server and tool gets capability labels: <code>reads_private_data</code>, <code>ingests_untrusted_content</code>, <code>external_egress</code>, <code>destructive</code> and <code>code_exec</code>. The labels come from code and config evidence, tool annotations and descriptions, and a list of well-known servers. Components that can be active in the same agent session are grouped, and dangerous combinations are reported as a path.</p>

  <Trifecta />

  <p>For example:</p>
  <blockquote><p>In one claude-desktop session: fetch can bring untrusted third-party content into the conversation; filesystem can read private data; fetch can send data outside. Instructions hidden in that untrusted content could make the agent read private data with filesystem and send it out with fetch.</p></blockquote>

  <FlowPath />

  <p>The cross-component lethal trifecta is HIGH by default, because almost every multi-server setup has one. It becomes CRITICAL when data can reach a known exfiltration sink, or when no hop requires approval.</p>

  <h2>Rug pulls: lock and verify</h2>
  <p>A static scan sees a server as it is today. <code>agentguard lock</code> writes <code>agentguard.lock</code>; <code>agentguard verify</code> compares a later scan with it and shows each change as a diff.</p>

  <LockVerify />
</Doc>
